# Copy to .env -- docker-compose.yml reads these. Never commit the real file.
#
# > [!IMPORTANT] Do NOT use FINDERR_-prefixed names in this file
# > Bun auto-loads .env from the working directory, and the app reads FINDERR_* straight
# > out of the environment. A FINDERR_ key here is therefore not inert: it configures the
# > app for every `bun test` and `bun run` on the HOST, outside the container entirely.
# > Compose maps each unprefixed name below onto its FINDERR_ variable.
# > `src/lib/env-hygiene.test.ts` fails the suite if a FINDERR_ key appears here.

# --- Radarr and Sonarr. The keys are REQUIRED; the app refuses to boot without them.
# Find each under Settings -> General in the respective web UI.
RADARR_API_KEY=
SONARR_API_KEY=

# --- Where the arrs live. Defaults assume finderr shares a Docker network with them;
# set these to LAN addresses (http://10.0.0.10:7878) if it does not.
#RADARR_URL=http://radarr:7878
#SONARR_URL=http://sonarr:8989

# --- Root folders, AS THE ARR SEES THEM rather than as finderr does.
#RADARR_ROOT_FOLDER=/media/movies
#SONARR_ROOT_FOLDER=/media/tv

# --- Quality profile ids. 4 is HD-1080p on a default install; check yours.
#RADARR_QUALITY_PROFILE_ID=4
#SONARR_QUALITY_PROFILE_ID=4

# --- Where an ADMIN'S BROWSER reaches each arr, when that is not where finderr does.
# The "Open in Radarr" link on a title page is built from this, and it is the only place
# in finderr that hands a browser an upstream URL -- so it is sent to admins ONLY, and it
# has to be an address a browser can actually resolve. Unset means "same as the URL above",
# which is right for a LAN-only instance and wrong once finderr is on the internet.
#RADARR_PUBLIC_URL=https://radarr.example.com
#SONARR_PUBLIC_URL=https://sonarr.example.com

# --- The per-episode mirror, which is what makes a series page say which episodes you
# already hold and lets you ask for a single one.
#
# Sonarr answers /episode for ONE series at a time, so this costs one request per series
# and not one per pass. Each library refresh walks a batch of the stalest series rather
# than all of them: on a 600-series library, walking everything on the 60-second timer
# would be ~600 requests a minute forever. Defaults fill a cold mirror in about half an
# hour and then settle under two calls a minute.
#
# Set the batch to 0 to turn the episode mirror off entirely.
#EPISODE_REFRESH_SECONDS=21600
#EPISODE_REFRESH_BATCH=25

# --- TMDB. OPTIONAL. Every facet that ships works keylessly through the Servarr
# proxies; this buys the two things nothing keyless carries -- streaming availability
# and keywords for series. https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
TMDB_API_KEY=
#
# Trim the cached streaming-availability facet to these countries. UNSET KEEPS EVERY
# COUNTRY, which is the default and what you want unless the cache size is a problem:
# TMDB returns ~112 countries per well-distributed film at roughly 25 KB, and a reader
# only ever sees their own. Set this ONLY if you know where your readers are -- a reader
# in a country you leave out loses the "Where to watch" row entirely rather than seeing
# a worse one. This is deliberately NOT FINDERR_REGIONS, which scopes the upcoming sync
# and defaults to US.
#TMDB_WATCH_PROVIDER_REGIONS=US,GB

# --- Plex. OPTIONAL. Without it a title's Play button is absent and nothing else
# changes. The token is FULL ACCOUNT ACCESS -- finderr only reads, but the token does
# not know that. It travels as a header, never a query parameter.
#PLEX_URL=http://plex:32400
PLEX_TOKEN=

# --- Auth. finderr is invite-only; the first boot prints an invite link for the first
# admin account.
#
# > [!CAUTION] AUTH_RP_ID IS PERMANENT -- set it before the first passkey is registered
# > It is the relying-party id every credential is bound to. Change it afterwards and
# > every passkey ever registered stops verifying, for everyone, with no migration short
# > of re-inviting every user. Use the BARE DOMAIN finderr will finally live at: no
# > scheme, no port. Leave it unset only while nobody has registered one yet.
#
# A passkey also cannot be created over plain http -- browsers require a secure context,
# and `localhost` is the only exemption. On a plain-http LAN the working door is
# "Continue with Plex"; the passkey buttons hide themselves and say why.
#AUTH_RP_ID=finderr.example.com
#AUTH_RP_NAME=finderr
# Comma-separated. EVERY origin that may complete a ceremony must be listed. The session
# cookie's Secure flag is derived from these: it turns on only when all are https.
#AUTH_ORIGINS=https://finderr.example.com,http://localhost:7979
# Lets an agent administer finderr without being a person: mint invites, manage users.
# OPTIONAL -- without it the admin API is reachable only by an admin's own session.
#
# It also unlocks the full /api/health payload. Anonymous callers get `{"ok":true}` and
# nothing else, because that endpoint has to stay open for the container probe and the
# detail (Plex machine id, library sizes, memory) is not for strangers.
ADMIN_API_KEY=

# Set to true ONLY when a reverse proxy YOU control terminates connections in front of
# finderr. It decides whether X-Forwarded-For is believed when identifying a caller for
# rate limiting, and it is wrong in both directions:
#   off behind a proxy -> every request shares one bucket, so the first abuser locks
#                         out every user and the limiter cannot tell them apart
#   on without a proxy -> the header is client-controlled, so an attacker has an
#                         unlimited supply of identities and the limiter never fires
#AUTH_TRUST_PROXY=false

# --- The assistant. OPTIONAL, and off unless you put a key here.
#
# No key means the feature DOES NOT EXIST rather than that it failed: no route, no button,
# no error message. Same shape as TMDB above -- a keyless install is a finderr that knows
# how to do one thing less, and nothing else changes.
#
# > [!CAUTION] What people type here LEAVES THE HOUSE
# > Questions go to OpenRouter and on to whichever provider it routes to. That is a fact
# > about the feature rather than a risk to be mitigated, which is why the audience is
# > deliberately small by default -- see AI_ADMIN_ONLY.
#
# The key travels in an Authorization header and never in a URL.
#OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#
# Which models may be used, comma-separated, best first. Benchmarked 2026-09-04 on the
# advanced tier: z-ai/glm-5.3-flash answered 5/5 at $0.0006 a question, which is about
# 1,700 questions per dollar; anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5 is three times faster at 26x the
# price. A LIST so an admin can benchmark an alternative without a redeploy.
#
# > [!IMPORTANT] This list and AI_DAILY_LIMIT_USD are ONE decision, not two
# > The cap is a naive check with no reservation machinery, so the worst overshoot past it
# > is one conversation -- ~$0.003 on the default model, 0.3% of a $1 cap. On an expensive
# > model the same check leaks most of the limit. Change the model, re-check the cap.
#AI_MODELS=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
#
# USD per user per day. 0 is unlimited. A "day" is a day in the CONTAINER'S timezone rather
# than in UTC -- a budget that resets in the middle of your evening is the wrong budget --
# and a refusal always states the reset as a delta ("resets in 5h 12m"), never a wall-clock
# time you would have to convert. Admins are exempt from this and from nothing else.
#AI_DAILY_LIMIT_USD=1
#
# > [!CAUTION] THIS IS AN ADMIN-ONLY BETA. THERE IS NO SETTING HERE THAT WIDENS IT.
# > Not an oversight -- questions go to a third party, and the per-account opt-in that would
# > let each person decide for themselves is not built. A variable that opened this to every
# > account would ship the consent problem ahead of the consent mechanism. When the opt-in
# > lands, the switch lands with it.
#
# One consequence worth knowing: admins are exempt from the daily limit above, so while the
# audience is admins the ledger RECORDS spend without LIMITING it. Watch the ai_call table.

# --- The arr callback. Radarr and Sonarr push request state changes to
# /api/webhook/arr instead of finderr waiting up to thirty seconds to poll for them, and
# they carry one thing no poll can see: an import that is BLOCKED and needs a person.
#
# > [!CAUTION] Leave WEBHOOK_PASSWORD empty and the route refuses everybody. That is the
# > default and it is deliberate.
# > This is the one public route that CHANGES state -- an arr has no cookie and cannot be
# > given one -- so this password is the whole of its authentication. Everything still
# > works without it, just at the poller's pace.
#
# Generate one and put it in BOTH places: here, and in the Webhook connection you add in
# each arr (Settings -> Connect -> Webhook), which has its own username and password
# fields. There is no shared-secret field in that form, which is why this is basic auth.
#WEBHOOK_USERNAME=finderr
#WEBHOOK_PASSWORD=
# Second layer: refuse a webhook whose source is not on a private network. Off by default,
# because it is silently wrong for anyone whose arrs are not on RFC1918 space -- a tunnel,
# another site, a hosted arr. It reads the source address through AUTH_TRUST_PROXY above.
#WEBHOOK_LAN_ONLY=false
